>
  <p>Most studying stops at the point of "I have read this and it makes sense". The Feynman technique pushes one step further, to "I can explain this to someone who has never seen it". That gap is where exam marks are lost, and closing it is the whole method.</p>

  <h2 id="what">What it is</h2>
  <p>The technique is named after physicist Richard Feynman, who was known for explaining difficult physics in everyday language. The idea: understanding and the ability to explain simply are the same thing. If your explanation needs jargon and hand-waving, you have found a part you do not actually understand.</p>

  <h2 id="steps">The four steps</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Pick a concept</strong> and write its name at the top of a blank page.</li>
    <li><strong>Explain it in plain language</strong>, as if to a younger student. No notes, no textbook. Use examples and simple words.</li>
    <li><strong>Find the gaps.</strong> Where you stall, hand-wave, or reach for jargon, mark it. That is what you do not know. Go back to the source and learn only those parts.</li>
    <li><strong>Simplify and use an analogy.</strong> Rewrite the rough bits in the simplest words you can, tying new ideas to things the beginner already knows.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="why">Why it works</h2>
  <p>Explaining from memory is retrieval practice, which Karpicke and Blunt showed beats re-reading and even concept mapping for long-term learning. There is also a "teaching expectancy" effect: preparing to explain something to others makes you organise it more deeply. The technique bakes both into one simple loop.</p>

  <h2 id="example">A worked example</h2>
  <p>Suppose you are revising supply and demand. You explain it out loud, then stall at "equilibrium price". That stall is the signal. You read only that section again, then rewrite it: "the price where the number of things people want to buy exactly matches the number sellers want to sell". Build these explain-and-check sessions into your <a href="/tools/study-plan-generator/">study plan</a>, and run them in <a href="/tools/pomodoro-timer/">short focus blocks</a>. To combine it with testing yourself, see <a href="/guides/evidence-based-study-techniques/">active recall and spaced repetition</a>.</p>
</Guide>
